The code statements that lead to the bug are:
cache->hold--;
Reason:
When `cache->hold` is 0 but `cache->count` is still non‑zero, the unconditional decrement causes an unsigned underflow (wrapping to `UINT_MAX`). This keeps `hold` artificially high, so `aa_put_buffer()` never returns buffers to the global list, starving other CPUs and forcing repeated `kmalloc(aa_g_path_max)` allocations.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (cache->hold)
    cache->hold--;
Reason:
This guard prevents the underflow by only decrementing `cache->hold` when it is non‑zero. Correctly handling the case where `hold` is 0 avoids the wrap‑around, allowing the per‑CPU cache to work as intended and buffers to be returned to the global list when needed.

